<header><p class="st-kicker">emirate · Anatolia and the Levant</p><h1 class="st-title">Banu Ammar of Tripoli</h1><p class="st-meta">1070 CE – 1109 CE (approx.) · capital Tripoli</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">A family of Shi'i judges who made Tripoli an independent city-state, famed for its library, until the Crusaders took it in 1109.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>The Banu Ammar were a family of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/qadi">qadis</a> in <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/county-of-tripoli">Tripoli</a> under the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fatimid-caliphate">Fatimids</a>. Around 1070, as Fatimid control of the coast collapsed, the qadi Amin al-Dawla Abu Talib al-Hasan ibn Ammar made the city independent.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>The city prospered from trade, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sugar-plantation">sugar</a>, paper and olive groves, and the Banu Ammar founded a Dar al-Ilm, a house of learning with a famous library. They kept their independence by diplomacy with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/seljuk-empire">Seljuks</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fatimid-caliphate">Fatimids</a>. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jalal-al-mulk-ali">Jalal al-Mulk Ali</a> extended their rule along the coast, and in 1099 the emir of <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/county-of-tripoli">Tripoli</a> paid the passing <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/crusader-states">Crusaders</a> to spare the city.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>From 1102 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/raymond-iv-of-toulouse">Raymond of Saint-Gilles</a> besieged <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/county-of-tripoli">Tripoli</a> from a castle he built on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mount-pilgrim">Mount Pilgrim</a>. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fakhr-al-mulk-ibn-ammar">Fakhr al-Mulk</a> went to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/baghdad">Baghdad</a> in 1108 to seek aid in vain, and in July 1109 the city fell to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/crusader-states">Crusaders</a> and became the capital of the County of Tripoli. Later chroniclers claimed the library was burned.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/banu-ammar-of-tripoli.jpg" alt="Banu Ammar of Tripoli" width="320" height="330"><figcaption>Image: Al Ameer son · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Political_map_of_the_Levant,_circa_1085.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1070 CE – 1109 CE (approx.)</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/11th-century">11th century</a> – <a href="/centuries/12th-century">12th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>emirate</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>mercantile</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Tripoli</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Amin al-Dawla Abu Talib, Jalal al-Mulk Ali, Fakhr al-Mulk</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Twelver Shia Islam</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Arabic</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/lebanon">Lebanon</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Emirate of Tripoli, Banu Ammar</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=1090&amp;focus=banu-ammar-of-tripoli" aria-label="Banu Ammar of Tripoli on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="5591 931 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Banu Ammar of Tripoli lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M5911 1187L5811 1337M5911 1187L5911 1187"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M5811 1337h0M5911 1187h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5911 1187h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5911 1187h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks Tripoli. <a href="/atlas?year=1090&amp;focus=banu-ammar-of-tripoli">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>
